From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 17:07:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 105/115] fix(refinery): serve each priority band
 oldest-first-handoff, not newest-created (gcp-et7g.1)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Both find-work queries took .[0] of `gc bd list ... --limit=1 --json` with
no sort. This store's default order is priority ascending, then created_at
DESCENDING, so the server-side limit took the newest bead in the top band on
every pass: each band was LIFO, and old merge requests starved while newer
same-priority work kept arriving. winnow on 2026-09-24 saw 47 same-band
overtakes and a ~9h wait, and a witness nudge to reorder could not override
it because the agent merges whatever find-work returns.

`--sort=created` is not the fix: --sort takes a single key, and every single
key drops priority (measured on the parent, gcp-et7g). And created_at is the
wrong key anyway: queue fairness is about when a bead joined the queue, which
nothing recorded. So (Option C, approved 2026-09-27):

- mol-polecat-work submit-and-exit §8 stamps the handoff in the SAME update
  that names the refinery, never a later one: last_submitted_at on every
  handoff, first_submitted_at only when the bead has none. A rejected and
  resubmitted bead keeps its original stamp, which is what puts it back in
  its old place in line (resubmit-first stays implicit). The first stamp is
  written only when a read that returned the bead shows the key absent, so a
  transient read failure cannot overwrite an original stamp. The block sits
  between handoff-stamp sentinels so a test can run it; the auto_push=false
  halt is not a handoff and is not stamped.
- mol-refinery-patrol find-work: both queries fetch every candidate
  (--limit=0) and pick with
  sort_by(.priority, (.metadata.first_submitted_at // .created_at)).
  Filters, assigned-first/routed-second, and the claim read-back are
  unchanged. Unstamped beads fall back to created_at; the stamp uses
  created_at's exact UTC format so the two compare as one timeline.
- The refinery prompt's "Uncertain merge order" row now says find-work's
  order is the default and a deviation is deliberate and recorded in
  patrol-summary, instead of inviting ad hoc reordering.

Tests: the find-work stub now orders matches the way the store does before
applying --limit (it returned fixture order, which is why the defect
passed). New cases (a) FIFO, (a2) handoff key beats creation, (b) priority
stays primary, (c) unstamped fallback, (d) routed scan. Against the
8c983d75 block, (a), (c) and (d) fail; against a created_at-only sort, (a2)
fails. New test_polecat_handoff_stamp.sh extracts the stamp block and runs
it against a mutating gc stub: both keys set on first handoff in one update,
first byte-identical and last strictly later on resubmit, a pre-seeded first
kept, an unreadable bead not stamped fresh, the halt not stamped.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
